From 75c8b530bbaf24a3857b946a41d724191d5c4741 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JerryNee <ni12@illinois.edu>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 16:35:35 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Discover and accept AirPlay over Apple peer-to-peer

A receiver that is only published on the infrastructure interfaces depends on
the network operator: managed networks filter or proxy mDNS, and some isolate
clients from each other. Publishing over AWDL as well lets a sender reach the
receiver directly, with no network configuration and no device registration.

Two things are needed, and neither works without the other:

- Register with kDNSServiceFlagsIncludeP2P | kDNSServiceFlagsIncludeAWDL on
  kDNSServiceInterfaceIndexAny. Pinning the registration to awdl0 instead
  hides the service from clients using other peer-to-peer interfaces, which
  is why earlier IncludeP2P experiments looked like a dead end.

- Set SO_RECV_ANYIF on the listening sockets. A conventional BSD listener
  silently drops packets delivered over AWDL, so without it the receiver is
  visible in the sender's list but the service never sees the connection at
  all -- no bytes, no log line, just "unable to connect".

Both are opt-in so default UxPlay networking is unchanged:
UXPLAY_DISCOVERY_PROFILE selects the registration profile and
UXPLAY_AWDL_RECV_ANYIF enables the socket option.

Note that mDNSResponder publishes a randomized UUID hostname for
peer-to-peer registrations. That is expected Apple behaviour, not a fault;
the name resolves over the AWDL link.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
